<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isidore of Seville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 560–<a href="/years/636">636</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saint Isidore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isidore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> was archbishop of Seville from about 600 to 636 and the leading scholar of Visigothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His Etymologies, an encyclopedia of ancient learning, was one of the most widely read books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and he also wrote a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. His relics were taken to León in 1063, where the church of San Isidoro was dedicated to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville.jpg" alt="Isidore of Seville" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isidor_von_Sevilla.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church of San Isidoro in León, which received the relics of <b>Isidore of Seville</b> in 1063, houses the Pantheon of the Kings, whose twelfth-century Romanesque paintings are among the best preserved in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nobles were taught reading, writing, science, medicine, law and philosophy, as the Institutionum disciplinae confirms; King Sisebut wrote poems, one on astronomy, and <b>Isidore of Seville</b>'s Etymologies, an encyclopaedia of the age's knowledge, was read throughout medieval Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>All the bishops of Hispania gathered at the church of Saint Leocadia in Toledo for the council convened by the Visigothic king Sisenand, over which <b>Isidore of Seville</b> probably presided.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-5">5 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cartagena, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Spalis. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.6,-0.98&amp;year=609" aria-label="Cartagena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4525 811 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isidore of Seville"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.6,-0.98&amp;year=609">What was here in 609 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For among the ancients no one wrote history unless he had been present and witnessed what was to be described. For we understand what we see better than we do what we gather by hearsay.&#8221;</p><footer>Etymologies, book 1, chapter 41 (On history) &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/51511/pg51511-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Histories of the heathen do no harm to their readers where they tell what is useful. For many wise men have put past deeds into their histories for the instruction of the present.&#8221;</p><footer>Etymologies, book 1, chapter 43 (On the usefulness of history) &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/51511/pg51511-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Letters are signs of things, symbols of words, whose power is so great that without a voice they speak to us the words of the absent; for they introduce words by the eye, not by the ear.&#8221;</p><footer>Etymologies, book 1 &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/51511/pg51511-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Philosophy is the knowledge of things human and divine, united with a zeal for right living.&#8221;</p><footer>Etymologies, book 2 &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/51511/pg51511-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
